---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import Capture from '../components/Capture.astro'
import SurfaceLab from '../components/SurfaceLab.astro'
import { connectors, doc, packages, releases, repo, shot, subscriptions } from '../lib/content'
---

<Page>
  <section class="hero container" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open source · macOS, Linux, Windows</p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">An IDE <br />built around <br /><span>the agent.</span></h1>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-summary">
      <p class="hero-lede">
        Phosphor is a desktop app for the pi coding agent. Give every task its own agent and Git
        worktree, and watch each command, edit, and diff as it happens.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use the plans you already pay for: Claude, ChatGPT, Copilot, Kimi, or any API key pi
        supports.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="action action-primary" href="#install"
          >Install Phosphor <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a
        >
        <a class="action action-text" href="#work">How it works <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a
        >
      </div>
      <div class="hero-meta mono">
        <span>MIT licensed</span><span>No sign-up</span><a href="#built">Built in Phosphor ↓</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-proof">
      <Capture
        image={shot('subagents')}
        alt="One Phosphor lane running four sub-agents at once: a scout that has reported back, two workers editing and testing, and a researcher reading a spec, with the session sidebar beside it."
        caption="One lane, four agents working in parallel"
        provenance="Demo session · scripted agents"
        sizes="(max-width: 480px) 92vw, (max-width: 1422px) 90vw, 1280px"
        eager
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="why" class="section problem" aria-labelledby="why-title">
    <div class="container problem-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
        <h2 id="why-title">One agent is easy.<br />Five is a mess.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="problem-copy">
        <p class="problem-lede">
          Run a few coding agents in separate terminals and you lose track fast. Which one is
          waiting on you? Which branch is it on? What did it actually change, and did the tests
          really run?
        </p>
        <p>
          Phosphor gives each task a lane: its own agent, its own worktree, its own diff. The home
          screen sorts lanes into Waiting on you, Ready to merge, Needs a push, In review, and
          Running, so you know where to look next.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="container">
      <Capture
        class="problem-board"
        image={shot('home')}
        alt="Phosphor’s lane dashboard with parallel sessions, a ready-to-merge task, and project usage."
        caption="Lanes from Phosphor’s own development"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="work" class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">01 / How it works</p><h2>
            Start with<br />a chat.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Type what you want done. Every tool call and edit shows up as it happens, and each chat
          runs on its own branch.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Capture
        class="workflow-chat"
        image={shot('chat')}
        alt="Phosphor’s chat-focused layout with a session sidebar, conversation, inline edit, and composer, with no auxiliary pane open."
        caption="Chat first, with the agent’s work one click away"
      />
      <div class="numbered-notes">
        <article>
          <span>01 / ISOLATE</span><h3>One task, one branch.</h3><p>
            Open a folder, pick a model, and the session starts in its own Git worktree. Start a
            second task and it gets a separate checkout.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article id="transcript">
          <span>02 / INSPECT</span><h3>See what it actually did.</h3><p>
            Expand any tool call for its arguments and output. Open an edit for its diff. Review
            every changed file against where the session started, and revert what you don’t want.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article id="artifacts">
          <span>03 / KEEP</span><h3>Plans and docs stick around.</h3><p>
            Plans, diagrams, and HTML pages the agent writes are saved as versioned artifacts.
            Reopen the session later to read, edit, or compare versions.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="source-line">
        <a class="text-link" href="/guides/#first-task">Run your first task →</a><a
          class="text-link"
          href={doc('lanes.md')}>How lanes and PRs work ↗</a
        ><a class="text-link" href={doc('extensions.md#the-artifact-tools-and-what-each-one-costs')}
          >How artifacts work ↗</a
        >
      </div>
      <SurfaceLab />
      <details class="disclosure">
        <summary>Changes and artifacts <span>+</span></summary><div class="capture-pair">
          <Capture
            image={shot('changes')}
            alt="An inline edit with the Changes pane beside the conversation."
            caption="Review the agent’s edits"
          />
          <Capture
            image={shot('artifacts')}
            alt="A document in the versioned artifact pane beside its conversation."
            caption="Artifacts open next to the chat"
          />
        </div><p class="fineprint">
          PR status comes from the gh CLI. Merges happen locally with git, not through GitHub.
          Artifact HTML runs isolated with no network access. That isolation covers the page, not
          the agent’s tools.
        </p>
      </details>
      <p class="fineprint" id="sessions">
        Resume old sessions, fork a conversation, or queue a follow-up while the agent is still
        working. Recurring work can run as a <a class="text-link" href={doc('routines.md')}
          >local routine</a
        >: a new session on a schedule, on your machine while it’s awake, not in the cloud.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="models" class="section alternate">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">02 / Models and accounts</p><h2>
            Use the plans<br />you already pay for.
          </h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          Sign in with a subscription, add an API key, or point at your own endpoint. Switch models
          mid-session without losing the conversation.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="subscription-grid">
        {
          subscriptions.map(({ name, plan, route }) => (
            <article>
              <>
                <h3>{name}</h3>
                <p class="mono">{plan}</p>
                <p>{route}</p>
              </>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <div class="split closeup feature-detail">
        <div class="copy-column">
          <h3 class="subheading">Switch models<br />mid-session.</h3><p>
            Search every model you’re signed into, across providers, with its context size and
            price. Pick a different one for the next turn and the conversation carries over.
          </p><p>
            The chip names what actually serves the session, so you can tell your Claude
            subscription (<code>pi-claude-cli</code>) from API-billed Anthropic.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Capture
          image={shot('models-picker')}
          alt="The model menu in the composer: a search box, provider filters, and models with their context size and price."
          caption="Every model you’re signed into, in one list"
          provenance="Real session · cropped"
          sizes="(max-width: 760px) 94vw, 560px"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="split closeup closeup-flip feature-detail">
        <div class="copy-column">
          <h3 class="subheading">See what a<br />session costs.</h3><p>
            The context meter shows tokens and cost as reported, an estimate of what’s filling the
            context, and how much of your Claude or ChatGPT plan is left.
          </p><a class="text-link" href={doc('chat.md#what-the-context-meters-popover-shows')}
            >How the context meter works ↗</a
          >
        </div>
        <Capture
          image={shot('context-meter')}
          alt="The context meter popover: context use by category, MCP servers, token and cost totals, and Claude plan usage."
          caption="Context, cost, and plan usage for one session"
          provenance="Real session · cropped"
          sizes="(max-width: 760px) 94vw, 560px"
        />
      </div>
      <aside class="boundary-note">
        <strong>More than one Claude account?</strong><p>
          Add every Claude login you have. New sessions can use one account, go down the list in
          order, or rotate round robin. A running session stays on the account it started with. <a
            class="text-link"
            href="/guides/claude-code/">Set up Claude and multiple accounts →</a
          >
        </p>
      </aside>
      <p class="fineprint">
        Claude subscriptions need the Claude Code CLI and the pi-claude-cli package. Your provider
        bills you for subscriptions and API usage. Phosphor itself is free. <a
          class="text-link"
          href="/guides/#sign-in">Pick a sign-in route →</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="extensions" class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">03 / Extensions</p><h2>
            Coding works out of the box.<br />Install the rest.
          </h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          pi runs the agent loop. Packages add tools on top: web search, MCP services, subagents,
          computer use. Phosphor installs, updates, and configures them from Settings.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="package-grid">
        {
          packages.map(({ name, benefit, id }) => (
            <a class="package-card" href={`/guides/extensions/#${id}`}>
              <>
                <span class="mono">OPTIONAL PI PACKAGE</span>
                <h3>
                  {name} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
                </h3>
                <p>{benefit}</p>
              </>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <div class="source-line">
        <a class="text-link" href="/guides/extensions/">Set up extensions →</a><a
          class="text-link"
          href="https://pi.dev/packages">Browse pi packages ↗</a
        >
      </div>
      <aside class="boundary-note">
        <strong>Built in vs. installed</strong><p>
          Artifacts, the context breakdown, and a few other extensions ship with Phosphor. The
          packages above are optional: install them in Settings → Extensions, then start a new
          session. Packages run code with your permissions, so check what you install.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="connectors" class="section alternate">
    <div class="container">
      <span id="gateways"></span>
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">04 / Connectors</p><h2>
            Your tools,<br />in the same session.
          </h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          Install the MCP adapter and connect the services you want the agent to use. No more
          pasting tickets and logs into the chat.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="connector-grid">
        {
          connectors.map(({ name, use, id }) => (
            <a href={`/guides/connectors/#${id}`}>
              <>
                <h3>
                  {name} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
                </h3>
                <p>{use}</p>
              </>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <Capture
        image={shot('guide-connectors')}
        alt="MCP Connectors settings with a connected-server list and the catalog of available services, using example configuration."
        caption="Add, sign in, test, or reconnect from Settings"
        provenance="Settings demo · example data"
      />
      <div class="source-line">
        <a class="text-link" href="/guides/connectors/">Connect your first service →</a><a
          class="text-link"
          href="/guides/connectors/#custom">Add a custom MCP server →</a
        >
      </div>
      <p class="fineprint">
        Setting up and testing a connector doesn’t call a model. Using its tools in a task does, and
        may count against the service’s quota. The adapter stores OAuth tokens, not Phosphor. Slack
        needs your own registered Slack app.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="built" class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Built in the open</p><h2>Phosphor is built<br />in Phosphor.</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          The lane board under “The problem” is real work on this repo. Every green merge to main
          that changes the app ships a release for all three platforms, and most installs update
          themselves.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="guide-grid proof-grid" id="feature-index">
        <a href={repo}><h3>Source ↗</h3><p>All of it, MIT licensed. Issues and PRs are open.</p></a
        ><a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/README.md`}
          ><h3>Technical docs ↗</h3><p>
            How each part works today, updated in the same commit as the code.
          </p></a
        ><a href={doc('known-issues.md')}
          ><h3>Known issues ↗</h3><p>What’s broken right now, written down.</p></a
        ><a href={releases}><h3>Releases ↗</h3><p>Every build, with checksums.</p></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="faq" class="section alternate">
    <div class="container faq-layout">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p>
        <h2>Questions<br />people ask.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>What is pi, and do I need it? <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <p>
            Yes. pi is the coding agent and Phosphor is the desktop app around it. Every session is
            a real pi process, so your pi setup, extensions, and sessions carry over. Install it
            with npm first (Node 22.19 or newer).
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary
            >How is this different from Claude Code or Codex in a terminal? <span aria-hidden="true"
              >+</span
            ></summary
          >
          <p>
            A terminal gives you a scrolling log. Phosphor shows each tool call with its output,
            each edit as a diff, and every file the agent changed since the session started, with a
            revert button. Each task runs in its own worktree, and one screen shows which tasks need
            you.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary
            >Can I use my Claude or ChatGPT subscription? <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary
          >
          <p>
            Yes. ChatGPT Plus and Pro sign in through pi. Claude Pro and Max run through the
            official Claude Code CLI with the pi-claude-cli provider, and you can add more than one
            Claude account. GitHub Copilot and Kimi For Coding work too, and so do API keys and
            custom endpoints. <a class="text-link" href="/guides/#sign-in">Sign-in routes →</a>
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What does it cost? <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <p>
            Nothing. Phosphor is free and MIT licensed. You pay your model provider, the same as you
            would without it.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary
            >Does the agent ask before running commands? <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary
          >
          <p>
            No. pi runs tools with full permissions, and Phosphor doesn’t add prompts of its own.
            Worktrees keep branches apart, not files or network access. If you want a confirmation
            before risky commands, install a permission gate. The repo has an optional one, and
            Phosphor shows its questions in a review sheet. <a
              class="text-link"
              href={doc('extensions.md#optional-permission-gate-and-scratch-cleanup')}
              >The optional gate ↗</a
            >
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does my code leave my machine? <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <p>
            Phosphor runs on your computer and needs no sign-up. Your model provider gets the
            prompts and tool results you send it, and connected services see what the agent asks
            them for. Your sessions don’t go through any Phosphor server. <a
              class="text-link"
              href="/guides/#trust">Permissions and data →</a
            >
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How many agents can I run at once? <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <p>
            As many as your machine can hold. Each live session is its own pi process, about 170 MB
            when idle, and nothing closes idle ones for you yet. Right-click a lane and choose
            Suspend to free that memory.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does it run on Windows and Linux? <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <p>
            Yes. macOS gets a DMG, Linux an AppImage or .deb, and Windows an .exe installer. Windows
            builds aren’t code-signed, so SmartScreen warns you on the first install.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is there a cloud or remote version? <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
          <p>
            Not yet. Everything runs on your machine. Local routines can start sessions on a
            schedule, but only while your computer is awake.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="install" class="section install-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">Start here</p><h2>Install it.<br />Give it a real task.</h2></div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          Two commands on macOS or Linux. Phosphor needs pi on your PATH, so install pi first.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ol class="install-steps">
        <li>
          <span class="mono">1 / pi, the agent · Node 22.19+</span><pre
            tabindex="0"
            aria-label="Install pi"><code>npm install -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="mono">2 / Phosphor · macOS and Linux</span><pre
            tabindex="0"
            aria-label="Install Phosphor on macOS or Linux"><code>curl -fsSL {`${releases}/latest/download/install.sh`} | sh</code></pre>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="fineprint">
        On Windows, run the .exe from the <a class="text-link" href={`${releases}/latest`}
          >latest release</a
        >. The script checks release checksums, but it’s still a script piped into your shell, so <a
          class="text-link"
          href={`${repo}/blob/main/tools/scripts/install.sh`}>read it first</a
        > if you’d rather.
      </p>
      <div class="source-line">
        <a class="action action-primary" href="/guides/">Full install guide →</a><a
          class="text-link"
          href={`${releases}/latest`}>Download the latest release ↗</a
        >
      </div>
      <div class="guide-grid">
        <a href="/guides/claude-code/"
          ><h3>Claude Code + accounts ↗</h3><p>
            Install the provider, sign in, test it, and route new sessions.
          </p></a
        ><a href="/guides/extensions/"
          ><h3>Choose your extensions ↗</h3><p>
            What’s built in, what to install, and how packages load.
          </p></a
        ><a href="/guides/connectors/"
          ><h3>Connect your services ↗</h3><p>
            MCP setup, permissions, sign-in, testing, and custom servers.
          </p></a
        >
      </div>
      <p class="fineprint">
        MIT licensed. Runs on your machine, not as a hosted service. The agent’s tools have full
        permissions by default, and your providers see whatever you send them. <a
          class="text-link"
          href="/guides/#trust">Permissions and data →</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
